{"summary": "I fixed `merge()` in `intervals.py`. It now merges intervals that share an endpoint or touch, as the docstring says.

The bug was the check `s < out[-1][1]`. It only merged intervals that overlapped strictly, so `[1, 3]`, `[3, 4]` stayed separate, and so did touching ones like `[1, 2]`, `[3, 4]`. The check is now `s <= out[-1][1] + 1`, so both cases become one interval.

I added five tests to `test_intervals.py`: shared endpoint, touching, a real gap kept apart, unsorted input with duplicates, and empty input. `python -m unittest` passes all 6 tests."}
